  "final_text": "I've done all four steps and read each result back from Kibana to confirm it.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' (id `d3d7af60-4c81-11e8-b3d7-01146121b73d`) points at the index `kibana_sample_data_flights`. Its time field is `timestamp`, which is a date field. The other fields are:\n   - **Numbers:** `AvgTicketPrice`, `DistanceKilometers`, `DistanceMiles`, `FlightDelayMin`, `FlightTimeMin`, `dayOfWeek`, `hour_of_day`\n   - **Yes/no:** `Cancelled`, `FlightDelay`\n   - **Locations:** `OriginLocation`, `DestLocation`\n   - **Text:** `Carrier`, `FlightNum`, `FlightTimeHour`, `FlightDelayType`, and the origin and destination fields for airport ID, city, country, region and weather.\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-51966962 Flights' (id `356b7b21-42eb-493a-8224-2e0fbdaaa2c6`) has exactly two bar-chart panels, both using that data view:\n   - \"Flights over time\" counts flights over `timestamp`.\n   - The second panel counts flights for the top 10 values of `Carrier`.\n   - I set the dashboard's default time range to the last 7 days.\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-51966962 Cancelled flights' (id `d4ff93fa-0516-4aec-ab80-7cfa2bbd0811`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_flights` that runs every minute. It counts documents with `Cancelled: true` over the last 5 minutes, using `timestamp`, and fires when the count is more than 10. It was enabled when created, so I didn't need to enable it. It has no notification actions attached, so it will fire but won't send anything.\n4. **Edit and read-back:** The carrier panel is now titled \"Flights by carrier\". Reading the dashboard back shows it still has two panels, \"Flights over time\" and \"Flights by carrier\". Reading the rule back shows `enabled: true`, a 1-minute interval and the threshold settings above.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
